[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi,</p>\n<p>I am currently wanting to transcribe some basic proofs in lattice theory into Lean for usage in static program analysis. My ultimate goal is to eventually be able to prove the soundness of certain analyses, but of course, things must be taken one step at a time.</p>\n<p>I believe I have a workable understanding of the differences between lean as a functional programming language and lean as an interactive proof assistant. My largest gap, though, I feel, is their usage together. For example, I would like to be able to parameterize some theorem for proofs on partial orders to rely on the <code>Ord</code> typeclass, and then use properties of that typeclass in my proof, but looking at the documentation related to this typeclass, I see nothing immediately obvious like innate semantic properties of this typeclass that could be useful in tactic mode. This is admittedly very possibly some tunnel vision on my part.</p>\n<p>An example I'm chewing on is the following:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Ord</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"n\">cases</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">compare</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">left</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">gt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">right</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Even more simply:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lt_is_le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Ord</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I have high confidence that Mathlib already has some tech in place to write goals relevant to lattice theory, but these proofs seemed simple enough to me to be a good introduction to transcribing what I might get practical usage from, outside of the Lean games online. I also anticipate monotone frameworks being easier expressed in terms of <code>Ord</code>, but I may be wrong.</p>",
        "id": 577799826,
        "sender_full_name": "Ash Hunt",
        "timestamp": 1772817526
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>Ord</code> doesn't have any properties by itself. Does <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=LawfulOrd#doc\">docs#LawfulOrd</a> exist?</p>",
        "id": 577807230,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruben Van de Velde",
        "timestamp": 1772819885
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Your goal isn't true since the LT and LE instances don't necessarily have to be related.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">False</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lt_is_le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Ord</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">solve_by_elim</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 577807252,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1772819894
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>LawfulOrd</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This seems like something with batteries, should I continue my investigation there?</p>",
        "id": 577807977,
        "sender_full_name": "Ash Hunt",
        "timestamp": 1772820115
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes</p>",
        "id": 577808861,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruben Van de Velde",
        "timestamp": 1772820390
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you want to formalize lattice theory you should presumably be using the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Lattice#doc\">docs#Lattice</a> typeclass, which gives you &lt; and ≤ and suprema and infima and proofs that they all behave as they should</p>",
        "id": 578161444,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1773064052
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I see that Lattice has instances that provide a Top and Bot (e.g. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/WithBot.html#WithTop.lattice\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/WithBot.html#WithTop.lattice</a>), but I'm confused on how to actually construct these.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y_is_upper_bound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Lattice</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Lattice</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Lattice</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Top</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y_is_upper_bound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The kernel does not like me for using <code>y : Top α</code> which is obviously distinct from <code>α</code>.</p>\n<p>I also recognize that <code>Lattice</code> by itself may be infinite, so a Top and Bot might not exist, which is what brought me to WithTop and WithBot, but I see no instance providing both.</p>\n<p>So I suppose if this is the most clear: I'm looking for a typeclass that provides Top, Bot, and is otherwise identical to Lattice. Help here is very appreciated and I apologize for the inconvenience, I'm finding it hard for whatever reason to navigate the documentation.</p>",
        "id": 578546749,
        "sender_full_name": "Ash Hunt",
        "timestamp": 1773205300
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It looks like I can construct a <code>Finite</code> <code>Lattice</code> <code>α</code>, but it's unclear to me currently from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Finite/Defs.html\">Finite Defs</a> how to construct the \"maximum\"; <code>Finite</code> doesn't immediately agree with <code>Lattice</code> and suddenly allow an easy Top term.</p>",
        "id": 578548102,
        "sender_full_name": "Ash Hunt",
        "timestamp": 1773206194
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Consider <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CompleteLattice#doc\">docs#CompleteLattice</a> , and also in your example consider:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Lattice</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Top</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y_is_upper_bound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">top</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>but ultimately this is misunderstanding what <code>Top</code> is, its the type class  for the notation <code>⊤</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Lattice</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Top</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y_is_upper_bound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 578549265,
        "sender_full_name": "Yan Yablonovskiy 🇺🇦",
        "timestamp": 1773206995
    }
]